 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB, you need to add an extra step to your existing code. Here's how you can modify the code to apply a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function before applying the Sobel operator:
```matlab
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_filter = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation of 3 pixels
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```
In this modified code, we first read the input image and convert it to grayscale as before. Then, we apply a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function with a standard deviation of 3 pixels. After that, we define and apply the Sobel operators in the x and y directions as before, but now we apply them to the gaussian_filter instead of the original gray_image. Finally, we combine the edges in both directions and display the resulting edge image.
This modification will help reduce the noise in the image before applying the Sobel operator, which should result in better edge detection results.

